course: dictionary
lesson: 8551
title: Spotting a scam
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A scam is a dishonest trick. Someone uses it to get your money or your private information, such as a password or card number. «Scam texts about parcels, prizes and unpaid bills trick many people every year into giving away card details.»
explain: You can use scam as a noun or a verb. As a noun, it is the trick itself: «Giveaways asking for card details are scams.» As a verb, to scam someone means to cheat them with such a trick.
explain: Scams often arrive as messages, emails or texts. «Real police do not ask for payment by message or phone.» Even a famous face can be used by a scammer, so a friendly or exciting message is not always a safe one.
explain: Some scams have special names. «Phishing means tricking someone into giving private details like a login or card number.» Scam texts are sometimes called smishing.
fact: A scam is a dishonest trick to get money or personal information.
fact: To scam someone is to cheat them with a trick like this.
fact: «Scam texts about parcels, prizes and unpaid bills» trick many people every year.
fact: Money lost to a scam is often gone for good, so stop and think before you pay.
fact: Never share passwords, codes or addresses, never click strange links, and always tell a trusted adult.
exercise: What does scam mean? | A secret password | A very long email | *A dishonest trick to get money or personal information | A friendly way to share a prize
exercise: «Giveaways asking for card details are scams, even if a famous face seems to promise them.» Why can a famous face still be part of a scam? | Because famous faces are always real | Because famous people never give things away | Because cards are famous too | *Because a famous face can be faked, so it does not prove the message is real
exercise: Jayden says, «If something seems too good to be true, it probably is.» He gets a message promising a free prize if he gives his card number. What should he do? | *Tell a trusted adult and do not share the card number | Share the card number with all his friends | Type in the card number quickly before the prize runs out | Click every link to check it
test: What is a scammer? | A person who helps people find lost parcels | A person who sells ice cream | A kind teacher who gives out prizes | *A person who tricks people to get money or private details
test: Which of these should you never share, according to online safety? | Your favourite colour | *Your password | Your favourite food | Your favourite film
exam: Which sentence uses scam as a verb? | *Criminals tried to scam my gran out of her savings | Scam messages can look very real | A scam text arrived on my phone this morning | The scammer was caught by the police
